The objective is precision-first. Accuracy, F1, Youden's J, and AUC are not the objective. Among thresholds that pass the gates, the rule keeps the highest `HIGH` recall. If recall ties, the larger threshold wins. Candidate thresholds are the unique ready residual values, quantized to 10 decimal places and sorted ascending. `YES` means residual greater than or equal to the candidate. The direction gate is the already frozen `direction_result`: median difference, rank-biserial, and AUC must all point the same way. It does not require a bootstrap interval to exclude zero.

The floors were chosen before any calibration labels existed. They are not a function of the development AUC. Calibration needs at least 12 ready `HIGH` rows and 8 ready `SECONDARY` rows. Predicted `YES` support on the comparison set must be at least 8, so one correct prediction cannot pass. Precision must be at least 0.80. The uncertainty method is the Wilson score interval at 95 percent, with the same z as `selection_surface.wilson_interval` (`1.959963984540054`). The lower bound must be at least 0.50. Zero support has no interval and fails the gate. Deleting any one comparison row must leave those precision, Wilson, and support gates intact, so one row cannot be the sole reason a threshold passes. One `REJECT` or `QUARANTINE` call of `YES` can still veto a threshold. These are not the development counts of 28 and 11.

